# Local development environment secrets for Cloudflare Workers (wrangler dev)
# Copy this file to .dev.vars to configure your remote or local Turso database credentials.
#
# Never commit a real .dev.vars. In deployment these values are set with
# `wrangler secret put <NAME>` and are deliberately absent from wrangler.jsonc `vars`.
#
# Turso Database URL:
# - Cloud database: libsql://myself-db-<org>.turso.io
# - Local Turso server ('turso dev'): http://127.0.0.1:8080
TURSO_DATABASE_URL="libsql://myself-db-<org>.turso.io"

# Turso Database Authentication Token (optional when using local 'turso dev')
TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN="your-turso-auth-token"

# Better Auth session signing secret (generate with `bunx auth secret`).
# Required in staging and production: auth refuses to issue sessions without it.
AUTH_SECRET="replace-with-a-random-32-byte-secret"

# Public base URL of this API, used to build verification and reset links.
# Defaults to http://localhost:8787 when unset.
AUTH_URL="http://localhost:8787"

# SameSite policy of the session cookie (S2): lax (default), strict or none.
#
# `lax` is what every environment gets unless this is set, and it is the right value when
# the web target and this API share a registrable domain — including the local setup,
# where both are on localhost. `/v1/auth/*` is called cross-origin but same-site there,
# so the cookie is sent and nothing has to be loosened.
#
# `none` is only needed when the web target and the API are not on the same registrable
# domain (for example an EAS Hosting web build calling a Worker on *.workers.dev): the
# cookie then has to travel cross-site, and `SameSite=None; Secure` is the only
# combination a browser accepts. Those two go together here: `none` is refused at startup
# in an environment whose cookie is not `Secure`, so it can only be selected in staging or
# production, where the cookie is always Secure. Nothing silently half-works: a browser
# drops `SameSite=None` without `Secure`, which would look like a session that logs itself
# out.
#
# Opting in means the browser no longer withholds the session cookie on cross-site
# requests, so CSRF rests on Better Auth's own origin check: every credentialed request
# carries an `Origin` that must match `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` (trustedOrigins) above — the
# reason that list must name the web origin exactly and never be left empty.
# AUTH_COOKIE_SAME_SITE="lax"

# Origins allowed to call this API from a browser (S15, S22, S26), comma-separated and
# explicit. Never a wildcard: the web session is a cookie, and a credential-bearing
# response is only readable when the origin is named. It is also what Better Auth trusts
# as `trustedOrigins`, so an origin missing here is answered with 403
# "Origin not allowed" (before the request reaches any handler).
#
# Local development: `make dev-web` serves the app on port 8081, and the Playwright suite
# on 8082. The API does not read this file on its own (`bun run dev` uses the process
# environment), so export the value or run with `bun --env-file=.dev.vars`:
#   export CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS="http://localhost:8081,http://localhost:8082"
# Deployed environments must set it to the deployed web origin(s) — the EAS Hosting URL
# of the web build plus any extra client origin. Native clients send no Origin and are
# unaffected by this setting.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS="http://localhost:8081,http://localhost:8082"

# Google sign-in (S1). Both values are required to enable the provider; when either
# is missing the provider is not registered and the API still boots, so leaving these
# commented out is the normal state for a checkout without Google credentials.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="<google-oauth-client-id>.apps.googleusercontent.com"
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="<google-oauth-client-secret>"

# Password of the single admin identity the seed creates (S20).
# The seed runs only when `ENVIRONMENT` is not "production" — the exact condition in
# seed.ts is `process.env.ENVIRONMENT !== "production"`, so an unset variable counts as
# non-production. It skips the admin identity whenever this is unset, printing one
# warning that names this variable. There is no default: the repository deliberately
# carries no admin credential, so a deploy that seeds without ADMIN_USER_PASSWORD has no
# admin and nothing can write curated content until the seed is re-run with it. Generate
# a strong, unique value per environment and set it with
# `wrangler secret put ADMIN_USER_PASSWORD` where the Worker needs it.
ADMIN_USER_PASSWORD="replace-with-a-strong-admin-password"

# Email sender (S16). No vendor is chosen yet, so the only implementation is the
# development one: it prints the verification and password-reset link and writes the
# whole message to a file, which is what lets a developer finish a reset without a mail
# client. It needs no API key and no account.
#
# It refuses to be constructed in a deployed environment (R1-002): both links carry a
# live single-use token, and a Worker log or a local file is not a delivery channel. In
# staging and production the API therefore boots with no sender and one warning, its
# verification and password-reset email are disabled, and completing a reset is still
# wired to revoke every session of the user. Wire a real vendor in
# apps/api/src/services/email/index.ts to turn both flows back on there.
#
# Envelope sender a real vendor would require (a verified domain). The development
# sender only records it; it defaults to no-reply@myself.local.
EMAIL_FROM="no-reply@myself.local"

# Directory the development sender writes messages to. Absent means the system temp
# directory (myself-emails/), which is deliberate: a message holds a reset token and must
# never land in the repository. Any directory outside the worktree works.
# EMAIL_OUTBOX_DIR="/tmp/myself-emails"
